  "response": "## What the scan reports\n\nOne `singular_plural_type_mismatch` finding in the whole report — `crime_00024`, file `src/api/state.ts`, lines 24–27, severity `low`, confidence `0.7`. Its evidence, verbatim:\n\n```\n\"plural name, singular type: `users: User` @L24\",\n\"singular name, array type: `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27\",\n\"v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped\"\n```\n\nSummary, verbatim: `\"2 declarations whose plural form disagrees with the annotated type's array shape.\"`\nFix shape, verbatim: `\"make the name agree with the type — `users` for array, `user` for scalar\"`\n\nBoth flagged declarations are in the same file, so I read it (`src/api/state.ts:24` and `:27`) rather than judging from the finding alone.\n\n---\n\n## 1. `users: User` @L24\n\n```ts\nexport const users: User = { id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" };\n```\n\n**Proposal: (a) rename — the type carries the intended meaning.**\n\nThe type annotation and the initializer agree with each other and outvote the name: `{ id: \"u_1\", email: \"a@b.c\" }` is one record, not a collection. Nothing here wants an array — turning it into `User[]` would mean rewriting the value too, i.e. inventing a requirement the code doesn't show. The name is the only artefact that says \"many\", and it's the cheapest thing to change.\n\nThe complication is the naming space. `src/api/state.ts:33` already declares `export const user: User = { id: \"u_2\", email: \"x@y.z\" };` — the detector's own suggested singular is taken. So rename to a qualifier that says *which* user, e.g. `currentUser` or `primaryUser`, rather than to bare `user`. Two same-typed module-level singletons with near-identical names is itself a smell worth resolving; if one of them is dead, delete it instead of renaming.\n\n## 2. `invoice: Invoice[]` @L27\n\n```ts\nexport const invoice: Invoice[] = [];\n```\n\n**Proposal: (a) rename — again the type carries the intended meaning, but the evidence is weaker.**\n\n`= []` is only assignable to the array type, so annotation and initializer are consistent; the singular name is the outlier. Unlike L24, though, the empty literal is nearly evidence-free — an empty array is what you write both for \"a real collection that starts empty\" and for \"I haven't decided yet.\" So the honest reading is: the type side wins on the evidence available, not on strong intent.\n\nSame collision problem — `src/api/state.ts:34` already has `export const invoices: Invoice[] = [];`, identical type and identical value. So the plural rename would produce a duplicate. Two live options:\n\n- **Merge**: this declaration is redundant with `invoices` at L34; delete L27 and point callers at L34. Nothing in the repo imports `src/api/state.ts` (the file's own header says *\"fixture material — do not import it from real code\"*, and the finding reports `blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`), so this is a free move.\n- **Rename with a qualifier**: if it's meant to be a distinct set, name what distinguishes it — `pendingInvoices`, `draftInvoices`.\n\nOption (b) — changing the type to match the singular name — would need `Invoice | null` or `Invoice | undefined`, because `[]` can't initialize a bare `Invoice`. Only choose that if the slot genuinely holds \"the one invoice under edit,\" which nothing in the file supports.\n\n---\n\n## Two things to carry into the edit\n\n**Fix the contract before the name.** Both types in this file are flagged as split-brain duplicates of `src/billing.ts`. From `crime_00011`, verbatim:\n\n```\n\"A: `Invoice` (interface) — src/api/state.ts:9, 2 field(s)\",\n\"B: `Invoice` (interface) — src/billing.ts:19, 11 field(s)\",\n```\n\nand from `crime_00010`:\n\n```\n\"A: `User` (interface) — src/api/state.ts:4, 2 field(s)\",\n\"B: `User` (interface) — src/billing.ts:4, 5 field(s)\",\n```\n\nRenaming variables that point at the wrong-shaped local `Invoice` just makes a stale contract read more confidently. Unify against `src/billing.ts` first, then re